\cf0 Jim, KD0NQQ and MAARS Secretary, said that I talked faster than he could\
write when I described the contents of my "VHF Grab 'n Go" suitcase at\
the last MAARS meeting, so he asked for an itemization; here it is:\
\
My VHF suitcase, which I have taken when deploying to the EOC, the\
hospital, the Red Cross office, an emergency shelter, or similar places\
where I might need to operate/coordinate with other HAMs, contains the\
following items (in no particular order):\
\
1. pad of paper; marking & ballpoint pens; paperclips; city, county,\
\'a0\'a0\'a0and state maps; "Amateur Radio" and "Official Radio Vehicle" (thanks\
\'a0\'a0\'a0to W0EVJ) signs; HAM-frequency chart; numbered-radiogram list and\
\'a0\'a0\'a0message forms; Civil-Defense and Red-Cross stickers & flags; ....\
\
2. A small 12-volt AC power supply (because a "local" deployment is\
\'a0\'a0\'a0probably indoors and probably will have AC power) and a bag of\
\'a0\'a0\'a0AC-related items:\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* Short extension cord\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* Three-prong-to-two-prong adapter (i.e., a "cheater")\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* Three-way multi-outlet (a.k.a. "Xmas tree")\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* Light-bulb-to-two-AC-outlets-plus-bulb adapter\
\
3. One or more 12-volt batteries (just in case!-) plus spare batteries\
\'a0\'a0\'a0(usually AA) for my HT\
\
4. A two-meter transceiver [plus a "handi-talkie" (HT) on my belt] and\
\'a0\'a0\'a0a small bag with transceiver microphone and power cable, plus adapters\
\'a0\'a0\'a0to get power from a cigarette lighter or straight from battery terminals\
\
5. A 12-volt extension cord\
\
6. some coax (never enough!-)\
\
7. a foldable DIY ground-plane two-meter antenna, plus rope to support\
\'a0\'a0\'a0it from a ceiling or tree and a gizmo to hang it from a "suspended"\
\'a0\'a0\'a0ceiling without leaving marks on the ceiling or support structure\
\
8. A bag containing a cheapie ($7, Harbor Freight) DVM & leads\
\
9. A broadcast-band FM radio, batteries, & earbuds in case duty is boring\
\
10. A bag with all sorts of 12-volt gender benders. \'a040+years ago I\
\'a0\'a0\'a0standardized all my radio power cords to use banana plugs and jacks,\
\'a0\'a0\'a0but the world moved on and other standards followed (the Molex male/\
\'a0\'a0\'a0female pair about 20 years ago, PowerPoles now), so my bag has:\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* a pair of BIG-alligator-clips-to-banana-plugs\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0(BIG enough to clamp to car-battery terminals)\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* cigarette-lighter-PLUG-to-Molex-female\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* cigarette-lighter-PLUG-to-pairs-of-banana-jacks\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* two-banana-plugs-to-cigarette-lighter-OUTLET\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* Molex-male-to-cigarette-lighter-OUTLET\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* two banana-plugs-to-banana-jacks\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* two banana-jacks-to-banana-plugs\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* old Comm-van-standard-to-Molex female\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* two one-banana-plug-to-three-banana-jacks (12-volt Xmas tree)\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* four two-banana-plugs-to-PowerPoles\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* two Molex-male-to-PowerPoles\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* two Molex-female-to-PowerPoles\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* one PowerPole-plus/minus-to-PowerPole-minus/plus adapter\
\'a0\'a0\'a0\'a0* Molex-male-to-LED polarity checker (VERY IMPORTANT!)\
\
11. A pair of headphones with quarter-inch-stereo plug\
\
12. A bag of 31 different audio gender-benders, including miniature- and\
\'a0\'a0\'a0sub-miniature mono- and stereo- to quarter-inch mono- and stereo-\
\'a0\'a0\'a0plugs and jacks, RCA to .... \'a0I really only need to carry enough to\
\'a0\'a0\'a0"bend" anything to the quarter-inch-stereo plug on my headphones,\
\'a0\'a0\'a0but I decided to store ALL of my audio gender-benders in the same\
\'a0\'a0\'a0bag instead of keeping a few in the bag and the remainder\
\'a0\'a0\'a0"somewhere" in my too-cluttered shack.\
\
13. A three-audio-inputs-to-one-stereo-outlet adapter which lets me\
\'a0\'a0\'a0listen to three audio sources at the same time (one goes to my left\
\'a0\'a0\'a0ear, one goes to my right ear, and one goes to both ears; this is\
\'a0\'a0\'a0especially useful at the EOC)\
\
I also have two other "Grab 'n Go" suitcases containing more radio-related\
stuff (HF transceiver, bigger power supply, longer coaxes, antenna makings\
[wire, insulators, golf-ball slingshot "launcher"], antenna tuner, scanner,\
AM and FM radios, etc. which I would grab for need-heftier-communication\
or will-last-longer deployments (although, to be honest and "just in case",\
I put ALL THREE in my vehicle for EVERY deployment beyond a trip to the EOC).\
\
I have another -- and much smaller -- suitcase (I think it was originally\
a "makeup bag") with packs of AA and AAA batteries and several FRS radios,\
in case I need to communicate over short (but longer than hollering)\
distances with non-HAMs. \'a0I grab this case when CERT calls.\
\
I do NOT normally pack any food, raincoats or other bad-weather clothing,\
blankets, candles, matches, etc., but -- since this is Kansas -- my\
vehicle already contains a small assortment of such stuff.\
\
